 185 ## 2. Contract proposal and product behavior
 186 
 187 Exact names and signatures are fixed in each phase spec. What follows is the
 188 contract each spec must realise.
 189 
 190 ### LH1 — a live producer over a caller root
 191 
 192 - **The protocol.** A producer is any object with
 193   `produce(call) -> dict`. `call` is a `ProducerCall`: the stage, the section
 194   ID, its source ID and version, its kind and span, the section's source bytes
 195   and their sha256, and the open questions that point at the section. The
 196   result is the response object the loop accepts today: `outcome` (one of
 197   `accepted`, `empty`, `refused`), `facts` and `proposal_keys`. The loop
 198   counts calls itself; a producer needs no `calls` attribute. `SavedProducer`
 199   is one implementation and keeps its pin and roster checks.
 200 - **A response is untrusted input.** The loop validates each response as a
 201   bounded JSON value of the declared shape before it uses it (standing
 202   rule 1). A response that fails, or a producer that raises, refuses the run
 203   with nothing written (D7).
 204 - **A caller root.** A root that holds `profile.json` is a caller root. The
 205   profile names the reading model file and its include files, and supplies
 206   what the loop takes from constants today: the facts added to every
 207   population, the producer's label, and the optional review, calibration,
 208   conflict and absence steps. A
 209   root without a profile is the packaged layout and keeps the packaged
 210   constants. A live producer over a root without a profile refuses. A
 211   declared calibration step takes its renderer label and the names of its two
 212   case folders from the profile; the packaged label, folder names and count
 213   of two are the packaged profile's values (H3) `(p1)`.
 214 - **Unavailable sections `(p1)`.** On a caller root the caller names its
 215   unavailable sections with an argument, and the manifest records them.
 216   `scenario="unavailable"` names the packaged section `study-methods`, so
 217   over a caller root it refuses before any producer call.
 218 - **Steps.** A step the profile does not declare is recorded `skipped`
 219   (R0.6). A declared step runs on the caller's files. A reviewer, if given,
 220   is called once per accepted response and must return a `supported` receipt;
 221   any other decision refuses the run, as a stale review does today. The
 222   reviewer is a check, not a filter (D3).
 223 - **Order.** Inputs are validated, then the reading model is extracted, then
 224   the first producer call is made (H7, standing rule 3).
 225 - **Route exits.** On a caller root the exits of `verify` and `check` over
 226   the broad and the targeted population are recorded as observed, 0, 1, 2 or
 227   4, and the run completes; any other exit refuses (D16, H23, H24) `(p1)`.
 228   The report shows the exits; it never calls a population verified. The
 229   packaged root keeps requiring 0.
 230 - **Per document.** `document_budget` bounds the bytes read from each source.
 231   It follows the global budget's rule `(p1)`: a section that does not fit in
 232   what is left of its document's budget is omitted, and a later, smaller
 233   section of the same document is still read (`reading.py:172-174`). Other
 234   documents continue. The report states a stop reason for each source, with
 235   the three reasons the global stop has. A document whose required questions
 236   are resolved still gets its other scheduled reads (D6, ruled `(o)`). It is
 237   a `run()` argument beside the global budget, with the largest-budget limit
 238   as its ceiling (D4).
 239 - **Limits.** The roster cap, the largest budget, the per-file input cap, the
 240   response cap, the route timeout, the route output cap and the retained-file
 241   cap are fields of one limits argument, each with a default equal to
 242   today's constant or today's effective bound (the retained-file cap of a
 243   `/2` run excepted, below), and a hard ceiling (D4, R0.8) `(p1)`. A JSON file is decoded under the cap it was read with, so a raised
 244   input cap also raises the decode cap of that input; the fixed 1 MiB decode
 245   cap of `depth.py:61` no longer applies to the reading loop.
 246 - **Retained files `(p1)`.** The packaged `/1` run keeps today's path: no
 247   bound before the first call, and replay reads its files through
 248   `reading._read(folder, name)` as today, so its bytes do not move (R0.2)
 249   `(p2)`. A `/2` run, before its first producer call, computes an upper bound
 250   for each file it will retain from the roster alone: a file that carries
 251   responses or receipts holds at most the sections eligible for it times the
 252   response cap plus a per-attempt overhead the spec fixes; any other file is
 253   bounded by the roster cap times a per-row size the spec fixes `(p2)`. With
 254   no retained-file cap given, the run's cap is the largest of those bounds,
 255   and never less than 128 KiB; the run refuses if that exceeds the
 256   retained-file ceiling, or if the bounds exceed a cap the caller gave
 257   `(p2)`. The manifest records the cap, and replay of a `/2` run reads under
 258   it through a reader local to `reading.py` `(p2)`. A run that completes can
 259   therefore always be read back.
 260 - **What a caller run writes.** The captured response table and the captured
 261   review receipts are artifacts of the run. Its manifest has format
 262   `literature-reading-run/2` and records the settings, the unavailable
 263   sections and each step's status (D5, R0.5). A packaged run with no new
 264   argument writes `literature-reading-run/1`, byte for byte (R0.2).
 265 - In LH1, `replay()` refuses a `/2` run by name; LH2 replaces that refusal.
 266 
 267 ### LH2 — replay of a live run
 268 
 269 - `replay(folder, fixture=ROOT)` reads the manifest, takes the settings and
 270   step statuses from it, reads the caller root through its profile, reads
 271   the run's files under the recorded retained-file cap `(p1)`, and
 272   re-derives every artifact. It has no producer, reviewer, budget or limits
 273   argument.
 274 - It refuses a response table, receipt table, stage, payload or input whose
 275   bytes differ from what the manifest binds, and a manifest whose settings
 276   exceed a hard ceiling.
 277 - A manifest binds a run internally. `replay(..., expected_evidence_sha256=)`
 278   compares the run with the digest `run()` returned, which the host keeps
 279   outside the folder; without it a consistently rewritten folder replays. The
 280   README says so. For the packaged run the independent producer pin keeps
 281   that role.
 282 - `score_reading_coverage` gains the same `fixture` argument and passes it
 283   through. Its 128-row cap stays (out of scope).
 284 
 285 ### LH3 — admission over a caller's assertion entity
 286 
 287 - **Shape.** The caller passes an assertion shape: the ID of an entity type
 288   in its model, and the entity types to follow from it (D17, ruled). With no shape,
 289   `admit()` runs the packaged shape and writes today's bytes.
 290 - **Attached facts.** A fact type is attached when one of its roles is played
 291   by the assertion entity. A row belongs to the assertion its role value
 292   names. Every attached row of an admitted assertion is kept, except a row
 293   that also names an assertion that was not admitted: the one limit stated
 294   under "Followed facts", which holds for attached and followed rows alike
 295   `(p2)`.
 296 - **Followed facts `(o)`.** Admission follows links outward (D9, ruled). When
 297   a kept row names an instance of a followed entity type, the rows of the
 298   fact types that entity plays a role in are kept for that instance too, and
 299   the walk continues from them. Example: a claim's row names quantity `Q7`;
 300   `Q7`'s value row and unit row are kept. The walk stops at the shape's
 301   list (D17, ruled): an instance of an entity type the shape does not list is
 302   kept as a value in the row that names it, and its own facts are not copied.
 303   With an empty list nothing is followed. One limit holds beside the list: a
 304   row that names an assertion which was not admitted is never kept, whichever
 305   route reaches it.
 306 - **Coverage.** The admitted population's `covers` lists the attached fact
 307   types and the fact types of the followed entity types. A fact type outside
 308   both is not copied.
 309 - **Identity `(o)`.** The assertion's own handle is its identity (D10,
 310   ruled). The model's preferred uniqueness is enforced by the stable
 311   commands, not re-implemented (R0.7). No rule links an objectified fact
 312   type's rows to the entity (H14): a shape that names an entity which
 313   objectifies a fact type refuses, naming the objectification. The packaged
 314   model's `assertion` is such an entity, so the packaged example stays on the
 315   packaged path.
 316 - **Judged unit.** Policy targets are rows attached directly to the
 317   assertion. An assertion is supported when at least one of its rows is a
 318   target and every target among its rows is admitted by
 319   `gnd judgments check` (D8). A target on any other row refuses.
 320 - **Optional facts.** An assertion with no row in an attached fact type
 321   simply has none. No value is read as "absent". The tag vocabularies and the
 322   condition rule of H16 apply to the packaged shape only.
 323 - **Row bound `(p1)`.** The generic projection bounds the rows it reads,
 324   attached and followed together, with its own `max_rows` argument, separate
 325   from the target cap (D18). The packaged projection keeps `MAX_TARGETS` as
 326   its row bound (`:46`), so its bytes do not move. `max_rows` without a
 327   shape refuses the call, since the packaged projection would not apply it
 328   `(p2)`.
 329 - **Include roots.** The snapshot copies each declared include beside the
 330   root, and the record binds each include's sha256 (H15).
 331 - **Record.** A call with any new argument (a shape, rules, `max_targets` or,
 332   with a shape, `max_rows`) writes `literature-assertion-admission/2`; the packaged call
 333   with none keeps `/1` (D12). A `/2` record binds the shape and the rules by
 334   sha256 of their canonical bytes, and records `max_targets` and `max_rows`
 335   `(p1)`. A call with rules and no shape is a packaged-shape `/2` call: it
 336   records the shape as `packaged` and `max_rows` as `null` `(p2)`.
 337 
 338 ### LH4 — admission rules as data
 339 
 340 - **Rules.** The caller passes admission rules: an ordered list of
 341   requirements, each with an ID, an attached fact type, a role, the
 342   admissible values and, optionally, a reason for each inadmissible value.
 343   An assertion satisfies a requirement when it has at least one row of that
 344   fact type and every such row has an admissible value. Requirements are
 345   evaluated in order, and the first one that fails gives the reason: the
 346   reason declared for the offending value, else the requirement's ID `(p1)`.
 347 - **Default rules `(p1)`.** The packaged comparison at `:122` becomes the
 348   default rules, evaluated by the one evaluator (R0.4): first `polarity` with
 349   `asserts` admissible and the reason `negated` for `negates`, then
 350   `attribution` with `own-finding` admissible and each other value as its own
 351   reason. List order keeps today's polarity-first precedence, and the
 352   reasons are today's strings, so the H18 stage table does not move.
 353 - **Disposition.** In a `/2` record, a supported assertion that fails a
 354   requirement has status `policy-refused` and that reason; an unsupported
 355   one keeps `fail` (D2). The `/1` record keeps today's statuses, so it keeps
 356   its bytes.
 357 - **A rule that names nothing refuses.** A requirement naming an unknown fact
 358   type or role refuses the call. It is never evaluated as "no row matched".
 359 - **The packaged shape `(p2)`.** There the assertion entity plays no role in
 360   the fact type that carries polarity and attribution (its roles,
 361   `assertion_admission.py:31-34`, have no `assertion`), so "attached" does
 362   not apply. On the packaged shape the one fact type a requirement may
 363   name is `assertion_statement` (`STATEMENT`, `assertion_admission.py:21`),
 364   which has one row per assertion, and the default rules name it. A
 365   requirement naming `assertion_names_work`, `assertion_names_passage` or any
 366   other fact type refuses there, as an unknown fact type does.
 367 - **Target cap.** `max_targets` is an argument from 1 to 1,024. The ceiling
 368   is the stable command's (H13, D11).
